Many data decline, Taiwan's economy has performed poorly in its opening year - the China-New Network.
On 11 February, the Xinhua Society in Taipei exported “five blacks” of its trade volume (journalist Zhao Bo, Huang Yang), the manufacturing procurement manager index (PMI) was retrenched for seven months, and the consumer price index (CPI) was again referred to as “three words” ... In the recent past, the Taiwan authorities have continued to publish economic data for January 2023.
The expert analysis found that the economy of Taiwan had performed poorly in its early years and that growth pressure was high.
According to the Taiwan authorities ' financial services authorities, the Taiwan region exported $31.51 billion in January this year, reducing it by 21.2 per cent a year, with negative growth for the fifth consecutive month.
Exports from all 11 major export categories declined compared to the same period last year.
Plastic rubber and its products and optical equipment suffered the sharpest decline, with 37.6 per cent and 35.7 per cent, respectively.
The export of electronic zero components decreased by 20.1 per cent annually, the largest reduction in 11 years.
Of these, integrated circuits have fallen by 18.3 per cent annually, the largest decline in 14 years.
The Chief of Statistics in this sector, Cheymena, stated that this showed a proliferation of weak demand for consumer electronics and that the electronics industry was entering the revision phase.
Statistics show a double drop in exports and imports from the Taiwan region in January, exceeding only $2.34 billion, the lowest level in nearly three years.
According to the Taiwan authorities ' financial services authorities, the decline in global end demand, the adjustment of industrial stocks inhibits the willingness of manufacturers to buy and exports are unlikely to improve in the short term, with Taiwan ' s export decline in the first quarter of this year threatening to be higher than expected.
According to the Taiwan China Economic Research Institute, the Taiwan Manufacturing Procurement Manager Index (PMI) was adjusted by season in January of this year to 40.4 per cent, a decline of 3.3 percentage points from last December and a contraction of seven months in a row.
In the system of indicators for economic monitoring, the PMI has a 50 per cent glorification line and an index of more than 50 per cent, representing an expansion of the climate and, conversely, a contraction.
Of the five PMI component indicators for Taiwan in January, new orders, production and manpower employment were tightened, supplier delivery times were reduced and stocks were tightened.
The six main industries have a total return on PMI contraction, with industries ranked by the rate of austerity as power and mechanical equipment (35.1 per cent), electronic and optical (35.2 per cent), chemical and biotechnical medicine (39.2 per cent), basic raw materials (42.4 per cent), transport (42.6 per cent) and food and textiles (45.7 per cent).
With regard to prices, the Taiwan authorities' “Main Master Plan” announced that the annual rate of increase in Taiwan's CPI in January of this year was 3.04 per cent, and that it has broken again by 3 per cent since July of last year.
Of this, vegetable prices rose sharply by 28.25 per cent, reaching a 17-month high.
In the words of Cao Chi Hoong, a special member of the Master Plan, the price of the food was indeed higher than expected, excluding the spring festival factor. The average CPI in January and February of this year should be close to 2.71 per cent last December, and inflationary pressures still exist and remain high.
Experts expressed pessimism when looking ahead to Taiwan's economy this year, combining the media reports from such islands as the joint newspaper, the Yahoo Economy and others.
Professor Wu Dae-jung of the Department of Economics of the “Central University” analysed that the export recession could cause problems in cash flows to export manufacturers and an increased risk of debt default.
At the same time, the reduction in orders led to reductions in the labour costs of producers, affecting the labour market and the household economy, which in turn affected consumption capacity.
According to Qiu Dasheng, a researcher at the Taiwan Economic Research Institute, the inflation situation in Europe and the United States has not been resolved this year, demand has continued to shrink and the decline in Taiwan ' s exports is expected to continue into the first half of the year.
Since the economy of Taiwan is export-subsistenced and is affected by low exports, the year-round economic growth will be less positive than last year.
